    "body": "The U.S. Department of Transportation announced $200 million in grants for 14 truck parking projects in 12 states. The funding will add or expand parking along major highways, near ports , and at a Texas border crossing, with improvements to lighting, security , and parking availability information.\nWhat’s Related\nTransportation Secretary Sean Duffy announced the awards last week. The department says 40% of truckers spend more than an hour a day searching for parking, and the grants aim to give drivers safer alternatives to highway shoulders and ramps.\n“They shouldn’t have to spend over an hour looking for a safe place to rest at the end of the day,” Duffy said.\nRELATED STORIES: Ranking the Lower 48 States by Truck Parking Availability | The 10 U.S. Cities Where Trucks Park on Ramps Most Often\nWhere the money is going\nThe grants cover new parking facilities and expansions at existing stops. Projects include:\nTennessee: Nearly $24 million for approximately 500 spaces at five Pilot Travel Centers along I-40 and I-24 near Nashville and Knoxville.\nGeorgia: $11.95 million for approximately 275 public truck spaces near the Port of Savannah in Garden City.\nNorth Carolina: About $6 million for approximately 140 truck spaces and 50 container staging positions at the Port of Wilmington.\nTexas: About $9.2 million for a project providing access to a new 305-space facility near the Los Indios Port of Entry, plus about $5.1 million for about 100 spaces at a Pilot Travel Center in Fort Worth.\nConnecticut: $13.5 million for up to 260 spaces along I-91 in Enfield, with bathrooms, vending areas, and parking availability monitoring.\nIowa: Approximately $24.5 million to expand and modernize six public rest areas along I-80.\nWisconsin: $15 million to add approximately 106 spaces at two rest areas along I-41 in Dodge County.\n“This investment will deliver hundreds of new spots across the country, helping hardworking truck drivers get the rest they need and reducing dangerous roadside parking on some of America’s most important corridors,” said Derek D. Barrs, administrator of the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration.\nOther awards will fund projects in Arkansas, California, Illinois, Minnesota, and Nebraska. Minnesota’s project will replace a rest area near Fergus Falls that closed due to recurring flooding, adding about 63 truck spaces along I-94.\nOther DOT news\nThe parking grants follow several other DOT announcements this year involving freight data , truck driver qualifications, and fuel deliveries :\nSharing freight data: The DOT announced the American Supply Chain Sovereignty Initiative , proposing a dashboard that connects ports , ocean carriers, trucking companies, railroads and retailers to help reduce cargo delays.\nStepping up enforcement: The department said its trucking safety crackdown removed more than 11,500 truck drivers from service and shut down 7,500 commercial driver’s license training schools.\nRemoving training providers: Federal regulators removed more than 110 truck driver training schools from the training registry and put more than 160 others on notice over training failures and links to English-language violations.\nEasing fuel delivery rules: A 90-day waiver temporarily eased hours-of-service rules for gasoline and diesel shipments , with required rest breaks and restrictions on which carriers could participate.",
